-The charged call is already paid for; the guard stops pi's agent loop from making more. Anthropic refusing to serve a request on plan limits is reported the same way.
+The charged call is already paid for; the guard stops pi's agent loop from making more. Anthropic refusing to serve a request on plan limits is reported the same way. A [fast](#fast-mode) call whose payload consents to usage credits is exempt: fast mode is billed to them by design. The exemption ends when the API reports it served the call at standard speed (`usage.speed`), also when that status arrives after a tool handoff, so a fast request Claude Code fell back on is guarded like any standard call. A call with no reported speed is taken as fast; that is not confirmation it ran fast.
+
+## Fast mode
+
+Claude Code's [fast mode](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/fast-mode) runs the same model faster at a premium price. On a Claude subscription it is **always billed to usage credits**, from the first token and even with plan allowance left, and needs usage credits turned on at claude.ai.
+
+The provider has no HTTP request of its own, so its payload is the launch: before every call it passes `{ model }` to pi's `onPayload` hook (and so to `before_provider_request` handlers). A hook that returns `speed: "fast"` gets the CLI launched with `--settings '{"fastMode":true}'`; adding `usageCredits: "allow"` consents to the charge (see [Usage-credit guard](#usage-credit-guard)). pi-extensions' Fast Mode `/fast` sets both.
+
+- Only Fable 5.2, Opus 5.5 and Opus 5 take it. They advertise it as model metadata, `serviceTiers: [{ id: "fast" }]`; every other model advertises none and runs at standard speed whatever the hook asks.
+- Speed is part of the launch signature: a change retires the parked process and the next call imports pi's history into a fresh one. Fast and standard keep separate prompt caches anyway.
+- Claude Code still decides: a disabled organization or exhausted usage credits run at standard speed. The `episode` span records the speed asked for (`speed=fast`) and whether a usage-credit charge was let through as fast (`rateLimit.fastConsent`).
+- A hook that throws fails the call, as on any provider.
 
